    {
      "title": "IPv6 Format and Why It Exists",
      "role": "outline",
      "outlineIndex": 4,
      "pages": [
        {
          "title": "IPv6 Format and Why It Exists",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "1. Format and Compression",
              "ink": "green"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "==128 bits== = eight groups of 4 hex digits, split by `:`",
                "Drop ==leading zeros== in each group",
                "Replace one run of zero groups with ==::==",
                "`::` is allowed only once"
              ],
              "style": "dot",
              "ink": "green"
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "lang": "text",
              "lines": [
                "2001:0db8:0000:0000:0000:ff00:0042:8329",
                "2001:db8::ff00:42:8329"
              ],
              "note": "same address, compressed"
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "2. Why It Exists",
              "ink": "blue"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "IPv4's ~4.3 billion addresses are ==exhausted==",
                "IPv6 gives ==2^128== addresses",
                "Enough public addresses, so ==no NAT== needed"
              ],
              "style": "check",
              "ink": "blue"
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "3. Prefixes and Checks",
              "ink": "purple"
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "headers": [
                "Prefix",
                "Meaning"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "`::1`",
                  "loopback"
                ],
                [
                  "`fe80::/10`",
                  "link-local"
                ],
                [
                  "`fc00::/7`",
                  "unique local (private)"
                ],
                [
                  "`2000::/3`",
                  "global unicast"
                ]
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "Typical subnet is a ==/64==",
                "`ip addr` or `ipconfig` shows your address",
                "`ping -6` tests IPv6",
                "No broadcast: it uses ==multicast=="
              ],
              "style": "dot"
            },
            {
              "type": "callout",
              "style": "sticky",
              "title": "Mistake",
              "text": "Using `::` twice makes the address ambiguous. Compress only ==one== zero run."
            },
            {
              "type": "sidebox",
              "title": "Interview Point",
              "items": [
                "**Why IPv6?** IPv4 ran out of addresses.",
                "**Can `::` appear twice?** No, once only.",
                "**Does IPv6 have broadcast?** No, multicast."
              ],
              "ink": "orange"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
